 <HI REND="ITALIC">anahaims, analaugnis, anasiuns, anavairþs;</HI>  5) cum
adverbiis:  <HI REND="ITALIC">analeiko, analaugniba, ananasiuniba.</HI></ITEM> <ITEM>ANAþAIMA (voc. gr.),  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajnavqema</FOREIGN> : Rom. IX, 3; Cor.
I, XVI, 22.</ITEM> <ITEM>ANAþOþ, nom. pr. masc.,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Anaqwvq</FOREIGN> : Esdr. II, 23.</ITEM> <ITEM>ANAKS, adv., subito, statim,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejxavpina</FOREIGN> : Mc. IX, 8;
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejxaivfnhû</FOREIGN> : Luc. II, 13; IX, 39.</ITEM> <ITEM>* ANAN (vet. norv.  <HI REND="ITALIC">önd,</HI>  suec.  <HI REND="ITALIC">anda,</HI>  dan.  <HI REND="ITALIC">aand,</HI> 
coll. gr.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>aænemoû,</FOREIGN>  lat  <HI REND="ITALIC">anima;</HI>  Bopp 126; Gf. I, 267),
conj. I:  <HI REND="ITALIC">on,</HI>  spirare.</ITEM> <ITEM>US-ANAN (praet.  <HI REND="ITALIC">uzon</HI> ), expirare, spiritum emittere,
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eJkpneiðn</FOREIGN> : Mc. XV, 37, 39.</ITEM> <ITEM>ANANEIAS et ANANIAS, nom. propr. masc.,
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Ananivaû</FOREIGN> : Neh. VII, 2.</ITEM> <ITEM>AND (vet. norv., angl. sax.  <HI REND="ITALIC">and . . . ,</HI>  vet. et rec.
sup. germ.  <HI REND="ITALIC">ant . . . , ent . . . ,</HI>  vet. fr.  <HI REND="ITALIC">and, ande, anda,
ond . . . , and . . . ;</HI>  Gf. I, 352; Rchth. 961, 604), praepositio
accusativum regens; ex omnibus cognatis sermonibus
in sola gothica et vetere Frisonum linguis,
ut vox separata occurrit, primario significans in, secus,
per, et ita usurpatur ut actionem non ad punctum
vel objectum individuale restringi, sed in toto
collectivo peragi significet, hinc haec particula vocibus
praeponitur, ut  <HI REND="ITALIC">orbi, terrae, regioni, localitati, civitati,
pago,</HI>  etc., quibus etiam adjectivum  <HI REND="ITALIC">alls</HI>  plerumque
praefigitur.</ITEM> <ITEM>I.  <HI REND="ITALIC">Praepositio:</HI>  1) in ordine ad  <HI REND="ITALIC">locum:</HI>  a) per, in,
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eijû,</FOREIGN>  post  <HI REND="ITALIC">usgaggan</HI>  Luc. XIV, 23; sic  <HI REND="ITALIC">þairhgaggan
and þata</HI>  ( <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejkeivnhû</FOREIGN> ) Luc. XIX, 4, et formula  <HI REND="ITALIC">and þana
laist</HI>  (proprie in vestigiis) Skeir. II, d; V a; b)
de,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>katav</FOREIGN>  c. genit., post:  <HI REND="ITALIC">run gavaurkjan</HI>  Mth.
VIII, 32, vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">rinnan</HI>  Luc. VIII, 33;  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diav</FOREIGN>  c. genit. post:
 <HI REND="ITALIC">athahan</HI>  Cor. II, XI, 33, et cum supplemento hujus
vocis Luc. V, 19; c) ad, per, in, cum objectum complexum
est,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eijû, katav</FOREIGN>  (c. accus. raro c. genit),  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejn</FOREIGN>  vel
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejpiv</FOREIGN>  c. accus.):  <HI REND="ITALIC">a</HI> ) communiter post verba motus
quorum subjectum est  <HI REND="ITALIC">meriþa, laiseins, vaurd</HI>  et similia,
sic post  <HI REND="ITALIC">urrinnan</HI>  Luc. IV, 14;  <HI REND="ITALIC">usgaggan</HI>  Mth. IX, 26;
Mc. I, 28; Luc. IV, 37; VII, 17;  <HI REND="ITALIC">galeiþan</HI>  Rom. X, 18;
post  <HI REND="ITALIC">gaþeihan</HI>  Skeir. IV, b; vel ubi subjectum ad
docendum (ut doctor,  <HI REND="ITALIC">merjandans</HI> ) iens, veniens,
etc., dicitur, ut post  <HI REND="ITALIC">vraton</HI>  Luc. VIII, 1;  <HI REND="ITALIC">galeiþan</HI> 
VIII, 39;  <HI REND="ITALIC">þairhgaggan</HI>  IX, 6;  <HI REND="ITALIC">qiman</HI>  III, 3; et inde post
 <HI REND="ITALIC">merjan</HI>  Mc. I, 39; XIV, 9; Mth. XI, 1;  <HI REND="ITALIC">gateihan</HI>  Rom.
IX, 17;  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>b</FOREIGN> ) in nonnullis aliis casibus ut  <HI REND="ITALIC">huhrus varþ</HI> 
Luc. IV, 25; XV, 14;  <HI REND="ITALIC">gasatjan and baurgs praizbytairein</HI> 
Tit. I, 5;  <HI REND="ITALIC">leikinonds and all</HI>  ( <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pantacouð</FOREIGN> ) Luc. IX,
6. Hoc pertinet etiam Cor. II, VIII, 18  <HI REND="ITALIC">þizei hazeins ïn
aivaggeljons and allos aikklesjons</HI>  ( <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diaú paswðn twðn eJkklhsiwðn</FOREIGN> )
et Eph. IV, 6  <HI REND="ITALIC">saei ufar allaim jah and allans</HI> 
 <COL N="1258"></COL> ( <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diaú pavntwn</FOREIGN> )  <HI REND="ITALIC">jah ïn allaim uns.</HI> —2) Ad  <HI REND="ITALIC">tempus</HI>  relata
valet ad,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>katav</FOREIGN>  (c. acc.), et semper habet  <HI REND="ITALIC">warjizuh</HI> 
juxta se positum: Mth. XXVII, 15; Mc. XV. 6.</ITEM> <ITEM>II. In  <HI REND="ITALIC">compositione:</HI>  1) cum verbis:  <HI REND="ITALIC">andbahtjan,
